# Line endings are a property of the repository, not of the machine that cloned
# it. Without this file Git normalises on checkout, so a Windows working tree
# gets CRLF and `prettier --check` (whose `endOfLine` defaults to "lf") fails on
# a couple of dozen files nobody has touched. `eol=lf` pins the working tree as
# well as the index, which makes `bun run format:check` give the same answer on
# every platform and in CI.
#
# Landing this file only changes what Git does from here on. The existing
# working tree still holds whatever line endings it was checked out with, so it
# needs a one-off `git add --renormalize .` to catch up.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets: never converted, never diffed as text. Git's own detection
# would catch these, but only after reading them; saying so is cheaper and
# survives a future asset it guesses wrong about.
*.ico binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.avif binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.mp4 binary
*.webm binary
